@reschke I listed the benefits [above](https://github.com/ietf-wg-httpapi/rfc7807bis/issues/67#issuecomment-1403213007)... In particular, the Link syntax natively supports internationalized parameters, and I gave one example of what that would look like.

There's also benefits _in general_... SF will bring more cases of problems resembling "impedance mismatches" and headers that require context-based parsing, which a pure ABNF solution would avoid from the beginning. Headers defined with an ABNF can be compressed more compactly than SF.

Some of my expertise is in parsing JSON, in languages for defining grammars like ABNF, and the overlap of the two (i.e. JSON Schema). If "SF Schema" is a phrase that makes you shudder, then I would like to share some research you'd probably be interested in.

@mnot Could you briefly explain what you mean? I suggested the Link syntax precisely to _avoid_ fragmentation (it's a well-established syntax).
